#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055804} :activity/id #uuid "09c039e8-49ea-5ece-8c46-7661967cfde9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050575 :topic/id #uuid "54fec5c0-eab2-5b02-8283-066ac6462c43" :topic/math-academy-id 138} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056008 :tutorial/content "To simplify an expression containing parentheses, we expand the parentheses and then collect like terms. \n\nFor example, let's simplify the following expression: \n\n$$\n3(x + 2) + 4x\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first step is to distribute the $3$ over each of the terms in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t3(x + 2) + 4x &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t3\\cdot x + 3\\cdot 2 + 4x &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t(3x) + (6) + 4x &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t3x +6 + 4x &\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe second and final step is to collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t3x + 6 + 4x &= \\\n\t\t(3x + 4x) + 6 &= \\\n\t\t7x + 6\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4f6f52d9-c069-5009-ae28-6eb58953a5cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8024 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "376c5b29-1d68-5736-b4c0-931cb44e0b98" :step/math-academy-id 52943 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054785 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054378 :question/id #uuid "f0bfc0e7-0db2-501d-a7b5-c7d14378ff85" :question/math-academy-id "e-12802"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e4e59ce-cc8a-5146-b78a-edbdd04909d0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050908}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445778}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Linear Expressions With Positive Numbers in Parentheses"} :step/id #uuid "2c6bec30-1031-5cef-a8a0-a25efef490a3" :step/math-academy-id 52944 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186